{"kind":"stack","id":"stk-materials","title":"Changes that make new materials","theme":{"background":"#fdf6e3","foreground":"#1d3557","font":"Georgia, serif"}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c1","kind":"flash","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c1-t","content":"Irreversible change"},{"type":"text","id":"c1-e","content":"A change that cannot be undone. The new material cannot easily turn back into the original."},{"type":"button","id":"c1-b","label":"Next Card","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c2"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c2","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c2-q","content":"When paper burns, what kind of change happens?"},{"type":"button","id":"c2-a","label":"A change that makes new materials","why":"Burning paper makes ash and smoke, which are new materials.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"sound","sound":"cheer"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c3"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c2-b","label":"A change that can be easily reversed","why":"Pupils may think burnt paper can be reused as paper again.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c3"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c2-c","label":"A change that only changes the shape","why":"Pupils may confuse burning with tearing or folding paper.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c3"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c3","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c3-q","content":"What is made when vinegar reacts with bicarbonate of soda?"},{"type":"button","id":"c3-a","label":"A new gas that bubbles up","why":"The fizzing is a new gas, carbon dioxide, formed in the reaction.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"sound","sound":"cheer"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c3-b","label":"Nothing new, just mixed together","why":"Pupils may think mixing two things never changes them.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c3-c","label":"Water that has frozen solid","why":"Pupils may confuse this reaction with a change caused by cooling.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c4","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c4-t","content":"You find a bag of bicarbonate of soda and a jar of vinegar. Which do you try first?"},{"type":"button","id":"c4-a","label":"Mix them in a cup","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c5"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c4-b","label":"Ask a teacher first","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c6"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c5","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c5-t","content":"The mixture fizzes and bubbles. A new gas has formed, and you cannot put it back in the jar."},{"type":"button","id":"c5-b","label":"Continue","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c7"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c6","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c6-t","content":"Your teacher shows you a safe demonstration. The fizzing shows that new materials have formed."},{"type":"button","id":"c6-b","label":"Continue","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c7"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c7","kind":"flash","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c7-t","content":"Reversible change"},{"type":"text","id":"c7-e","content":"A change that can be undone, such as melting ice or dissolving sugar in water and then getting it back."},{"type":"button","id":"c7-b","label":"Next Card","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c8"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c8","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c8-q","content":"Which change is usually irreversible?"},{"type":"button","id":"c8-a","label":"Baking a cake","why":"Baking changes the batter into a new material that cannot be unbaked.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"sound","sound":"cheer"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c9"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c8-b","label":"Melting a chocolate bar","why":"Pupils may forget that melted chocolate can cool and set again.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c9"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c8-c","label":"Stretching an elastic band","why":"Pupils may think stretching is a material change, when it returns to shape.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c9"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c9","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c9-q","content":"What new material forms when wood burns?"},{"type":"button","id":"c9-a","label":"Ash","why":"Ash is a new solid material left behind when wood burns.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"sound","sound":"cheer"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c10"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c9-b","label":"Sawdust","why":"Pupils may think wood only breaks into smaller pieces.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c10"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c9-c","label":"Sap","why":"Pupils may think sap is released from wood as it heats.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c10"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c10","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c10-q","content":"Is a candle burning a reversible change?"},{"type":"button","id":"c10-a","label":"No, the wax and air make new materials","why":"Burning creates new materials such as soot and gases that cannot be turned back.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"sound","sound":"cheer"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c10-b","label":"Yes, the wax can be poured back in","why":"Pupils may think melted wax is the whole story and forget the burnt parts.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c10-c","label":"Yes, the flame goes back into the wick","why":"Pupils may believe the flame is a solid thing that can return.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c11","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c11-q","content":"Which of these shows a change that makes new materials?"},{"type":"button","id":"c11-a","label":"Rusting an iron nail","why":"Rust is a new material formed when iron reacts with oxygen and water.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"sound","sound":"cheer"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c12"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c11-b","label":"Folding a sheet of paper","why":"Pupils may think folding changes the material, when only its shape changes.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c12"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c11-c","label":"Dissolving salt in water","why":"Pupils may think salt disappears, but it is still there and can be recovered.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c12"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c12","kind":"end","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c12-h","content":"Well done: you explored changes that make new materials."},{"type":"text","id":"c12-m","content":"Burning and acid reacting with bicarbonate of soda form new materials that cannot easily be reversed."}],"actions":[]}}
